+
+## Requirement trace
+
+- B8 (node manager cannot be retired), observation route. Production entry: daemon `RuntimeLoop` step (src/observe/step.rs:509 `DesiredRuntime::load`, which sets `node_manager` from `model::manager::live_node_manager`) then `classify`, then `build_ops`, then the `observed.cascade` mutation. classify drops the node manager seat and its live clones from workspace, tab and pane cascades and drops its teamspace from the workspace cascade `retire` list; `CascadeMutation` re-reads `live_node_manager` at apply time and skips that seat, its clones and its teamspace. Delivered. Tests: node_manager_tab_close_is_not_retired_and_reopens, node_manager_pane_close_is_not_retired, workspace_close_keeps_node_manager_teamspace_active, cascade_rechecks_node_manager_at_apply.
+- B11 (stays open, reopened if closed). The node manager's disappearances go to `gone`/`gone_ends`, so build_ops marks its runtime absent and ends occupancy, and the reconcile planner recreates the workspace, tab and panes. Delivered for closure; tests run settle and assert a new pane exists (assert_reopened). The "cannot be moved" half is promised by the spec only as tolerate-and-warn for observed moves (spec 4.9); classify emits a warning naming the node manager by name and id when its clone moves or its tab closes with its panes surviving elsewhere, and step.rs prints it with the observer's existing eprintln convention. Delivered per the spec's reading; the plan-side placement refusal belongs to the plan cluster.
+- B6 (reparent on retirement), observed route. `CascadeMutation` sets `Acc.lenient_reparent = true`, and every retirement goes through `do_retire_seat`, which calls `plan::managers::reparent`; rewrites and fallback notes land in the ClosureCascade action record (`reparented`, `reparent_fallback`). Delivered for the observed route; tests reparent_on_observed_cascade and reparent_fallback_on_integrity. Other retirement routes and the undo of rewrites belong to other clusters (seam reviewer).
+- Other seats in the node manager's tab or workspace still retire normally (spec 4.9): covered by node_manager_tab_close_still_cascades_other_seats and the workspace test, which asserts the action retires exactly bench and its clone.
+
+## Analysis
+
+classify: the three containment loops stay disjoint. A node manager tab or pane inside a closed workspace is absorbed by the workspace branch, and its panes are skipped through `absorbed_seats`/`ws_gone`, so nothing is ended twice. `diff::occupancy` only emits OccupancyEnded for panes that are still matched, so the new `gone_ends` entries do not duplicate an occupancy end. A clone of the node manager that was moved into another teamspace's workspace falls to the pane loop when that workspace closes, and the node manager check there keeps it unretired.
+
+mutations: the apply-time re-check is correct for reparenting. `reparent` computes the retiring set per seat from the overlay, so a skipped node manager stays a surviving manager and reports of other retiring seats still resolve to it.
+
+Apply-time race (classification before a concurrent `node-manager set`, apply after): the skip leaves the node manager's seat, clone and teamspace records exactly as they were. The runtime is not set absent and the clone occupant is not ended. build_ops already attached the classified `ends` to the cascade op, so commit_ops emits SessionEnded for the node manager's clone while the record still shows the occupant. The planner looks tabs and panes up by id against the snapshot, so a stale binding does not match and reopening should still happen, but no test exercises that path through settle. The window is narrow and the B8/B11 outcome holds, so this is recorded as deferred, not blocking.
+
+The ClosureCascade action is written even when the re-check skips everything (for example a tab cascade for the node manager alone in the race). write_action has always behaved this way for all-already-retired cascades; harmless.
+
+The move warning is per classification. It can repeat if a step's ops fail and the next diff re-derives the same move; the noise is cosmetic.
